    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the length of the longest side of the similar triangle, we first need to determine the perimeter of the original triangle and then use the concept of similar triangles to find the length of the corresponding side in the similar triangle.\n\nStep 1: Find the perimeter of the original triangle.\nThe original triangle has sides of lengths 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm.\n\nPerimeter of the original triangle = 6 cm + 7 cm + 9 cm = 22 cm\n\nStep 2: Determine the ratio of the perimeters of the two triangles.\nThe perimeter of the similar triangle is given as 110 cm. To find the ratio, divide the perimeter of the similar triangle by the perimeter of the original triangle.\n\nRatio of perimeters = 110 cm / 22 cm = 5\n\nStep 3: Apply the ratio of perimeters to the longest side of the similar triangle.\nSince the triangles are similar, the ratio of their corresponding sides will be equal to the ratio of their perimeters. We know the perimeter of the original triangle is 22 cm, and we want to find the length of one side of the similar triangle. The unknown length is the longest side, which has a perimeter of 110 cm.\n\nLet x be the length of the longest side in the similar triangle.\nx / 110 cm = 5 / 22\n\nStep 4: Solve for x.\nCross-multiply to get:\nx = (5 / 22) * 110 cm\n\nSimplify the equation:\nx = (5 / 22) * 110 = (5 * 110) / 22\n\nx = 550 / 22\n\nx = 25 cm\n\nSo, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is 25 cm.",
